Similar running shoes

Saucony Ride 19

Price: $145Weight (pair): 1 lb, 1.8 oz men's and 15.4 oz women's Heel-to-toe drop: 8 mmStack height: 36 - 28 mmCushion level: ModerateSupport type: Neutral

The Saucony Ride 19s are reliable, do-it-all daily trainers for road running that are among the best shoes for the value you can find. The PWRRUN+ midsole balances cushion with moderate responsiveness for a smooth, consistent ride at a wide range of paces. This has long been one of our favorite road running shoes.

Compared to the Rides, the Triumph is more plush and has a higher stack and drop. It’s more specialized for easier runs and recovery, though it certainly works well as a daily trainer too. Choose the Triumphs if you want to prioritize cushion and impact absorption; choose the Rides if you want a dependable, versatile daily trainer at a great value. Learn more in our in-depth review of the Saucony Ride 19 road running shoes.

HOKA Bondi 9

Price: $175Weight (pair): 1 lb, 5.0 oz men's and 1 lb, 2 oz women's Heel-to-toe drop: 5 mmStack height: 43 - 38 (men's), 42 - 37 (women's)Cushion level: MaximumSupport type: Neutral

The HOKA Bondi 9 is a maximum-cushioned road running shoe that, like the Triumphs, works well for daily training runs and active recovery. The Bondis are a little heavier than the Triumphs and have a supercritical EVA foam midsole. However, they’re very comfortable and have a more moderate 5mm drop, which may mean a smoother ride for most runners. These models feel pretty similar to each other in terms of comfort, cushion, support underfoot, and overall ride, so we recommend going with whichever feels most comfortable to you, or whichever you find at a better deal.

Learn more in our in-depth review of the HOKA Bondi 9 road running shoes.

Saucony Hurricane 25

Price: $170Weight (pair): 1 lb, 4 oz men's and 1 lb, 1.6 oz women's Heel-to-toe drop: 6 mmStack height: 38 - 32 mmCushion level: MaximumSupport type: Neutral

The Saucony Hurricane 25 is another maximum-cushioned road running shoe option from Saucony. Like the Triumphs, it is plush underfoot and works well as a daily training shoe or an active recovery shoe. The midsole is made from Saucony’s PWRRUN PB, like in the Triumph 23s, rather than incrediLUX, like in the updated Triumph 24s. This means the cushion may feel a touch more firm in the Hurricanes, but there is more of it.

We found the Hurricane 25s to feel extremely cushy and comfy, and ideal for recovery runs or easier daily training runs. Both shoes serve similar purposes, so we recommend choosing whichever feels more comfortable to you, or whichever one you find for a better deal.

Why you should trust us / About the author

Becca Downs ran D1 cross country and track for Indiana University—Purdue University of Indianapolis (IUPUI) and continued racing everything from elite miles to an ultramarathon in her post-collegiate years. She earned a USATF Level 1 certification and coached boys and girls high school cross country and track in Indianapolis for four years. Becca then joined the IUPUI women’s distance coaching staff as a volunteer assistant coach in 2020-2021. She also notched a few small FKTs under her belt.

She geeks out on trail running, track and field, and cries every time she watches a marathon (they’re just so beautiful!). She earned her MFA in Creative Writing from the Mile-High MFA Program at Regis University. Becca is based in Denver, Colorado.

